    "['ipv6 enable', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/2 unit 0 family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent command for `ipv6 enable`:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/2 unit 0 family inet6\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nNote:\\n1. Juniper uses a different interface naming convention (ge-1/0/2 instead of GigabitEthernet1/0/2)\\n2. The command enables IPv6 processing on the interface by adding the inet6 family\\n3. Juniper requires explicit unit 0 configuration (even if not used)\\n4. This is the basic equivalent - additional IPv6 parameters would need separate configuration\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/2 unit 0 family inet6'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 0 family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent command for `ipv6 enable`:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/2 unit 0 family inet6\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nNote:\\n1. Juniper uses a different interface naming convention (ge-1/0/2 instead of GigabitEthernet1/0/2)\\n2. The command enables IPv6 processing on the interface by adding the inet6 family\\n3. Juniper requires explicit unit 0 configuration (even if not used)\\n4. This is the basic equivalent - additional IPv6 parameters would need separate configuration\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 0 family inet6'}]]": 1,
    "['ipv6 address autoconfig', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'unit [unit_number] family [family_type]', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]'}]]": 2,
    "['ipv6 enable', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/2 unit 0 family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/2 unit 0 family inet6\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. In Juniper, IPv6 is enabled on an interface by configuring the `inet6` family under the interface's unit.\\n2. The `ge-1/0/2` and `ge-1/0/1` correspond to Cisco's `GigabitEthernet1/0/2` and `GigabitEthernet1/0/1` interfaces.\\n3. Juniper requires explicit unit numbering (typically unit 0 for physical interfaces).\\n4. This is the Juniper equivalent of Cisco's `ipv6 enable` command.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/2 unit 0 family inet6'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 0 family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/2 unit 0 family inet6\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. In Juniper, IPv6 is enabled on an interface by configuring the `inet6` family under the interface's unit.\\n2. The `ge-1/0/2` and `ge-1/0/1` correspond to Cisco's `GigabitEthernet1/0/2` and `GigabitEthernet1/0/1` interfaces.\\n3. Juniper requires explicit unit numbering (typically unit 0 for physical interfaces).\\n4. This is the Juniper equivalent of Cisco's `ipv6 enable` command.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 0 family inet6'}]]": 1,
